Please note that the above assertions are not in the same context as the original "PUT_InsertContract" method, so, it's a completely different problem where it's checking the invariants of the data structure.--------------------This program does not directly correspond to a piece of standard C# code that employs "Debug.Assert", but it uses assertions through the PexObserve and PexAssumeUnderTest tools to assert some criteria in the program. Therefore, if you want to convert this code to use "Debug.Assert", you should consider how these programmatic tools work in C#.

The PexObserve.ValueForViewing statements are used to capture some values and compare them later in subsequent runs or assertions. This might be achieved by running this code more than once and comparing some pre or post conditions that have been observed with the PexObserve. If it turns out that all these previous assertions fail, it suggests that these are not valid assertions and should be replaced with "Debug.Assert" statements.

These are the conditions that need to be checked with a assert statement so the corresponding PexObserve statements could be used.
- $new_arrList_Count is less than or equal to $old_arrList_Count
- $new_arrList_IndexOf_x is equal to $old_arrList_index
- $new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x is equal to $old_arrList_IndexOf_x
- $new_arrList_contains_x is true

Please note that this will only work if these statements are in a method that is being proved, otherwise you should add the `[PexMethod]` attribute. However, this would imply that the "PUT_..." function is being proved by Pex because it belongs to a class where the `[PexClass]` attribute is applied. In this case, you would still have to manually add the `[PexMethod]` and `[PexAssumeUnderTest]` attributes to the function.--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(index <= arrList.Count); 
// This check assumes that index is less than or equals to the original count of array list, which gives a guarantee that the element will be added in the correct position. 
// Reasoning: The index argument to the .Insert(int index, T item) method refers to an item's position in a list - array's indicies start from 0. So it's impossible to insert an item at the position greater than the 'Count' of array - i.e. the index cannot be greater than the last valid index in the array.  

Debug.Assert(index >= 0); 
// This check assures that the index is non-negative. Arrays in C# are zero-based, meaning index 0 is the first position in the array. Negative indexes are not possible, since they are not valid reference points in the array.

Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount <= arrList.Count); 
// This check asserts that count of the array list before the insert is still less than or equal to the new count of the list after the insert. 
// Reasoning: We are adding element 'x' at the given index, so the count after insertion needs to be equal to the count before insertion because element 'x' does not increase the array's count.  

Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= 0); 
// This check ensures that old index of x is 0 or greater. IndexOf method returns the index of the first occurrence of a specific object. It returns -1 if the object is not found in the array. 

Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0); 
// This check asserts that old last index of x is not less than 0. LastIndexOf method returns the index of the last occurrence of a specific object in the array. It returns -1 if the object is not found in the array.

Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0); 
// Same as last line, checks that new index of x is not less than 0.  

Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX < Old_arrListLastIndexOfX); 
// This assert statement verifies if last index of x is different before and after the insertion of element. 
// Reasoning: If last index of x remains unchanged or increases then all the assertions will still pass, which doesn't hold true in these cases. Lastindexof method returns the index of the last occurrence of a specific object in the array. So, when an element is inserted, it could potentially have shifted the location of the last occurrence of x. Hence this check.

Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX); 
// This checks whether 'x' is present after the insert operation. Assertion fails if false.
