The first assertion fails if the array list count is decreased after the 'arrList.Insert(index, value)' function call. The second assertion fails if 'IndexOf' doesn't return the same value as it did before the insertion. If x is not in the list, the third assertion will fail. The fourth one will also fail if 'Contains' doesn't return the same value before and after the insertion. These assertions could be useful for identifying the cause of a bug when these conditions are not met.--------------------```csharp
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX <= Old_arrListCount, "$last_position of x after insertion is greater than original array count");
Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX <= Old_arrListCount, "$last position of x after insertion is greater than original array count");
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount + 1, "$new list count is one more than old list count");
```
These assertions will check that the last positions of x after insertion are less than or equal to the original array count and also that the count of the new array list is one more than the old array list count.--------------------Based on the provided code snippet, it seems like the condition for the last position of x after insertion needs to be checked. However, since the last position doesn't seem to be stored in any variable, we need to calculate it manually. So here is the modified C# code:

```cs

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_InsertContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
        {
            // Assume conditions before modification
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index <= arrList.Count && index >= 0);

            // Store initial values
            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_index = index;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            // Modify the array
            arrList.Insert(index, x);

            // Store final values after modification
            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            // Capture the initial and final values
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            // PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

            // Assert condition for the last position of x after insertion
	    // The last position of x is the highest index in the array that has that value 
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListCount, "last position of x after insertion is less than or equal to new array count");
        }
    }
}

```

This modified code includes the necessary assertions to help you verify if the `New_arrListLastIndexOfX` (which is calculated manually from the array list) is equal to `New_arrListCount` or less, which corresponds to the comment "@@@ last position of x after insertion is less than or equal to original array count".--------------------The following "Debug.Assert" statement will check if the `New_arrListIndexOf_x` is less than or equal to `$old_arrList_Count`:
```C#
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOf_x <= $old_arrList_Count);
```--------------------Here is a C# code snippet that would create debug.Assert statements that match the comments in the code:

```cs

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_InsertContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index <= arrList.Count && index >= 0);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_index = index;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            arrList.Insert(index, x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX <= Old_arrListCount); 
            // @@@ last position of x after insertion is less than or equal to original array count
        }
    }
}

```
Debug.Assert can be used to impose conditions that must always hold true at every particular point of code execution.--------------------First, we need to import the `System.Diagnostics` namespace which contains the `Debug` class. Then we use `Debug.Assert` method on conditions based on comments in the code.

```cs
using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_InsertContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
        {
            // Assume condition from pex
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index <= arrList.Count && index >= 0);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_index = index;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            arrList.Insert(index, x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

            // @@@ last position of x after insertion is less than or equal to original array count 
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX <= Old_arrListCount);  
        }
    }
}
```
